---
// src/pages/bucknell/anop-330.astro  →  arizvi.com/bucknell/anop-330
// ANOP 330: the term week by week and 2 classes to try. Not linked from the nav; sends noindex.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import CourseHeader from '../../components/CourseHeader.astro';
import CourseNav from '../../components/CourseNav.astro';
import ThresholdDemo from '../../components/ThresholdDemo.astro';
import OverfitDemo from '../../components/OverfitDemo.astro';
import TermFigure from '../../components/TermFigure.astro';
import Assignment from '../../components/Assignment.astro';
import { a330 } from '../../data/assignments';
import { course } from '../../data/bucknell';

const c = course('anop-330');

// The term. Each week: the method, the business question it answers, and the class to try where there is one.
// Three units, each with a figure in the margin.
const units = [
  { n: 1, from: 1, to: 4, weeks: 'Weeks 1-4', name: 'Predicting, honestly', line: 'Framing, leakage, regression, and data the model never saw' },
  { n: 2, from: 5, to: 8, weeks: 'Weeks 5-8', name: 'Classifiers and their costs', line: 'Logistic regression, trees, ensembles, and the cutoff as a business call' },
  { n: 3, from: 9, to: 14, weeks: 'Weeks 9-14', name: 'In the world', line: 'Clusters, fairness, LLMs, monitoring, and the project' },
];
const weeks: { w: number; topic: string; build: string; try?: string }[] = [
  { w: 1, topic: 'Framing a prediction', build: 'What exactly are we predicting, could we know it in time, and what would we do differently? Python and pandas refresher, a first train/test split.' },
  { w: 2, topic: 'Data preparation and leakage', build: 'Features that would not exist at decision time. The lab hides a leak and the class finds it.' },
  { w: 3, topic: 'Regression, revisited', build: 'Linear regression with many features: reading it and predicting with it are different jobs.' },
  { w: 4, topic: 'Overfitting and cross-validation', build: 'Will it work next quarter? Holding data out, and holding it out 5 times, before any model gets trusted.', try: 'overfit' },
  { w: 5, topic: 'Logistic regression', build: 'Probabilities instead of labels, and the confusion matrix.' },
  { w: 6, topic: 'Decision trees', build: 'How a tree splits, and why it is the model a manager can read.' },
  { w: 7, topic: 'Ensembles', build: 'Random forests average many trees to steady the prediction; gradient boosting adds small trees one at a time to fix what the last ones missed.' },
  { w: 8, topic: 'Evaluation and thresholds', build: 'Precision, recall and the cost of each kind of mistake, with the cutoff chosen on held-out data. Then uplift: ranking customers by how much the offer would change what they do.', try: 'threshold' },
  { w: 9, topic: 'Clustering', build: 'k-means segmentation, choosing k, and groups we did not define.' },
  { w: 10, topic: 'Fairness and the loop', build: 'Who is harmed when it is wrong, and what a person signs off on before it ships.' },
  { w: 11, topic: 'LLMs in the workflow', build: "Embeddings and zero-shot classification as features. Where a model drafts and a person decides, picking up ANOP 102's week 8." },
  { w: 12, topic: 'Deployment and monitoring', build: 'Drift, and how we know when a model stops working. Every project ships with a model card.' },
  { w: 13, topic: 'Project work', build: "Each team's model, scored on a term of data the team never saw." },
  { w: 14, topic: 'Project presentations', build: 'Pitch a model to a decision-maker: what it predicts, what acting on it costs, and when to stop trusting it.' },
];
---
<Base title="ANOP 330" description="ANOP 330, Machine Learning Fundamentals for Business, as Ali Rizvi would teach a section at Bucknell - the term week by week, with 2 classes you can try." noindex>
  <CourseHeader course={c} facts={[
    ['Format', '1 credit · 14 weeks · 2 classes of 75 minutes a week'],
    ['Tools', 'Python in Jupyter: pandas and scikit-learn. ANOP 203 is the prerequisite, so code from week 1.'],
    ['Grading', 'Weekly labs 30% (pass or redo, with 5 late days) · midterm 20% (a timed notebook on a new dataset) · team project 30% · final 15% (on paper: read a model\'s output and decide) · model card 5%'],
    ['The project', 'Teams of 3 or 4. Data from a real organization where we can get it, public data otherwise. A notebook, a model card and a 2-page memo, scored on a held-out term of data. It goes on GitHub, with the organization\'s say-so where there is one.'],
    ['AI tools', 'Allowed for code and debugging. Hand in the prompts with the notebook, and say in the model card where AI was used.'],
  ]}>
    <p>ANOP 330 already runs in the department, and whatever outline it runs on comes first. Below is a term as I'd plan it if asked, and 2 classes I'd bring to a section whichever outline it follows. What I'd emphasise: the decision each model feeds, what its mistakes cost, and who signs off before it ships.</p>
    <p class="practice"><span class="label">From practice</span>At Amazon I owned Alexa Orchestration. The German word weiter means continue, and Fire TV understood it one way and Echo devices another: each mode had its own idea of what the word meant. I built a generic tool that disambiguates words like that across languages, automatically. Week 4 is where it starts: a model returns probabilities, and the system around it decides what to do with them.</p>
    <p class="label pack-link"><a href="/bucknell/anop-330/pack/">The full course pack: syllabus, every week, every lab, the rubrics and a sample final →</a></p>
  </CourseHeader>

  <Section label="A term, as I'd plan it" title="Week by week" id="term">
    {units.map((u) => (
      <div class="unit grid">
        <div class="unit-side">
          <TermFigure course="330" unit={u.n} />
          <p class="label">{u.weeks}</p>
          <h3>{u.name}</h3>
          <p class="unit-line">{u.line}</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="rows compact unit-weeks">
          {weeks.filter((x) => x.w >= u.from && x.w <= u.to).map((x) => (
            <li class="grid">
              <div class="label">Week {x.w}</div>
              <div class="body">
                <span class="title">{x.topic}</span>
                <p>{x.build}{x.try && <> <a href={`#${x.try}`}>{x.try === 'overtime' ? 'Read the class below.' : 'Try it below.'}</a></>}</p>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    ))}
  </Section>

  <Section label="Assignments" title="What is handed in, and how it is marked" id="assignments">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>Two of the assignments in full: the cross-validation lab from week 4, and the team project. Rubrics are published with the assignment, so students know what the marks are for before they start. The pitch video, the midterm, the model card, the paper report, all 10 labs and a sample final are in the <a href="/bucknell/anop-330/pack/">full pack</a>.</p>
    </div></div>
    {a330.map((a) => <Assignment {...a} />)}
  </Section>

  <Section label="Try a class · Week 8" title="The most accurate cutoff earns nothing" id="threshold">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>Your subscription business has 1,000 customers. Next month 100 of them will cancel - you just don't know which 100. A model gives every customer a risk score.</p>
      <p>You can send a retention offer. It costs $20, and each customer it keeps is worth $200. To keep the arithmetic simple, say it keeps everyone it reaches who was about to cancel. How many of your riskiest customers should get it?</p>
      <p>Start at nobody - that's the most accurate cutoff. Then slide right and watch the money.</p>
    </div></div>
    <ThresholdDemo />
  </Section>

  <Section label="Try a class · Week 4" title="The model that memorised the past" id="overfit">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>An ice-cream stand logged 24 days of sales and the day's temperature. 16 days are on the chart. The other 8 are in a drawer.</p>
      <p>Fit a curve to the 16. A straight line misses; a curve that bends can follow every bump. Pick the degree you'd ship by the error on the days you can see. Then open the drawer.</p>
    </div></div>
    <OverfitDemo />
  </Section>

  <CourseNav current={c.slug} />
</Base>
